-- title:
--   Supercuspidal type character at q has λ-power order
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a non-zero natural number and $g$ a weight $2$ cusp form on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform, i.e. a normalised eigenform (first $q$-coefficient $1$, multiplicativity of the $q$-coefficients at coprime indices, and the usual recursions for prime powers, distinguishing primes dividing $M$ from those not dividing it) such that no proper divisor $M'\mid M$ carries a normalised eigenform with the same $q$-coefficients at all primes not dividing $M$. Let $\lambda$ be a prime, $S$ a finite set of primes, and $O'$ a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with finite residue field, with $\lambda$ in its maximal ideal. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra $\mathrm{heckeAlgebra}\ M\ 2\ S$ (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of the space of weight $2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(M)$ generated by the $T_\ell$ with $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$ and the $U_p$ with $p\mid M$, $p\notin S$) to $\mathbb C$ sending each such $T_\ell$ to the $\ell$-th $q$-coefficient of $g$, let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the image of $\chi_g$ to $O'$, and let $\rho$ be a $\lambda$-adic Galois representation over $O'$: a free $O'$-module of rank $2$ with an adically continuous action of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$. Assume the residual representation $\rho.\mathrm{residual}$ (the base change to the residue field) has no invariant subspace other than $0$ and the whole space and sends every involution $\neq 1$ to an element of determinant $-1$, and that for every prime $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\ell$ a non-unit in $A$ and every $\sigma$ which is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $A$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\iota(\chi_g(T_\ell))X+\ell$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q\neq\lambda$, let $\Phi$ be a non-zero $\mathbb C$-valued function on adelic $\mathrm{GL}_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ of which $g$ is an adelic lift (left invariance under the global points, right invariance under the level-one subgroup attached to $M$, and the prescribed weight $2$ slash value at matrices with trivial finite part and totally positive archimedean part), and let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a non-unit, such that every element of the inertia subgroup at $P$ acting trivially on $\sigma z\, z^{-1}-1$ modulo the non-units, i.e. lying in wild inertia, is sent to the identity by $\rho$. Let $V$ be a $\mathbb C$-vector space with a $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q_q)$-action commuting with the scalars, whose subspace of vectors fixed by the congruence subgroup $\mathrm{gl2CongruenceSubgroup}\ q\ 1$ is finite-dimensional, and let $f$ be an injective $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q_q)$-equivariant linear map from $V$ to the adelic span of $\Phi$ whose image is the $\mathbb C$-span of the orbit of the distinguished element of that span. Let $\theta:\mathbb F_{q^2}^\times\to\mathbb C^\times$ be a character and assume the representation of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Z/q)$ on the fixed subspace of $V$ is cuspidal of type $\theta$: dimension $q-1$, no non-zero vector fixed by all upper unipotents, trivial central action, and the stated characteristic-polynomial identity on the non-split torus relating $\theta$ with the induced representation. Assume finally that $q^2$ exactly divides $M$ ($M.\mathrm{factorization}\ q=2$), that the residual representation, regarded as an adic representation over the residue field, has characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$ on inertia at every valuation subring above $q$, that $q\equiv-1\pmod\lambda$, and that $\lambda\neq 2$. Then $\theta\neq 1$ and $\theta^{\lambda^n}=1$ for some natural number $n$.
--
--   This is the local-type input to the level-lowering step at a prime $q$ exactly dividing the level to the second power: the type character of a depth-zero supercuspidal component is shown to be non-trivial of $\lambda$-power order, which is what rules out this component in the presence of residual unipotency on inertia at $q$. It feeds the analysis of eigensystems attached to semistable models with Steinberg-type quotients.
